ly. The company operates in three segments: Americas, International, and AECOM Capital. The company offers advisory, planning, consulting, architectural and engineering design, construction and program management services, and investment and development services to public and private clients in major end markets such as transportation, facilities, water, environmental, and energy. It is also involved in the investment and development of real estate projects. The company was formerly known as AECOM Technology Corporation and changed its name to AECOM in January 2015. AECOM was incorporated in 1980 and is headquartered in Dallas, Texas.","ceo_bio":"Mr. W. Troy Rudd | Chairman & CEO | Age 60 | Compensation: $3.9M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 51K people. Revenue per employee of $316K ranks in the below the sector median among Industrials peers, improving at 6%/yr \u2014 growing workforce leverage. Each employee generates $13K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $1K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 12\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (1/10).","financial_summary":["AECOM is a mid-cap industrials business with a $8.6B market cap on $16.1B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +7% over the past three years and -1% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 14% against a 8% cost of capital, a 5-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it, and 5% gross margins are typical of commodity economics where competitors can replicate the product cheaply.","The business throws off $685M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 4 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.46\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.9\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage runs at 5.6\u00d7.","Management returns a meaningful 5.6% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 6.0% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Significant insider selling (net $2.5M)","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","dollars":-2516632.95},{"text":"Gross margin trails most Industrials peers \u2014 competitors appear to have the pricing hand","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Industrials sector lagging the market significantly, underperforming by 8% over 3 months","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Industrials ETF trading well below its long-term trend","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Tariff and trade-policy exposure \u2014 industrial exporters sit squarely in the crosshairs of protectionist policy, and retaliatory tariffs can close markets without warning.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading at a 48% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","Trading near the bottom of its 52-week range \u2014 the market is offering a discount","Piotroski score 7/9 \u2014 fin